  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nWhile high temperatures today will remain 3 to 7, locally up to 12 \ndegrees above average they're forecast to be 3 to 5 degrees cooler \nthan yesterday. That cooling trend will continue through Tuesday \nor Wednesday for most locations. By Wednesday, high temperatures \nare expected to be 3 to 7 degrees below average for this time of \nyear. Additionally, marine layer low clouds and areas of fog will \nbecome more prevalent along the coast and in the western valleys \nat times. \n\nMoisture from Tropical Cyclone Karina may be pulled into Southern\nCalifornia by mid-week. There is high confidence this will \nincrease high cloud coverage across the area, with lesser \nconfidence in chances of light rain. Current forecast has pulled \nrainfall chances back for much of Wednesday, leaving the highest \nchances on Tuesday at 15-25 percent. Model guidance still is yet \nto align on the amount of moisture that moves into our area. The \nEC continues to trend drier than the GFS/Canadian. How the \ntropical cyclone tracks will ultimately be the biggest influence \nin moisture influx, with a more westerly track being drier for us\nand a more easterly track being wetter. Current forecast closely \nfollows the NBM. \n\nThursday into next weekend, the upper level high begins to shift \nback westward, bringing warming and the potential return of monsoon \nmoisture. High temperatures return to near average by Friday.",
